The distribution of the nail problem, just as in the skin, will provide some guide to the likely cause. If the abnormality is widespread and symmetrical, it is more likely to have an endogenous than an exogenous explanation.  The distribution of the nail problem, just as in the skin, will provide some guide to the likely cause. If the abnormality is widespread and symmetrical, it is more likely to have an endogenous than an exogenous explanation.  If just a few nails appear abnormal, particularly if these are toenails, consider whether the changes might be due to trauma or a fungal infection. What does the patient do to his/her nails—biting, manicuring, trauma at work? If infection is a possibility, you may wish to take nail clippings for mycological examination and culture (see E p. 646).  If just a few nails appear abnormal, particularly if these are toenails, consider whether the changes might be due to trauma or a fungal infection. What does the patient do to his/her nails—biting, manicuring, trauma at work? If infection is a possibility, you may wish to take nail clippings for mycological examination and culture (see E p. 646).  For more information about the causes of a nail dystrophy, see E p. 96. For drug-induced nail problems, see  pp. 380-1.  For more information about the causes of a nail dystrophy, see E p. 96. For drug-induced nail problems, see  pp. 380-1.

