Human papillomavirus (HPV) infects epithelial cells of skin and mucous membranes; $>150$ types of HPV have been identified. These cause infections at different sites (see Box 7.9). Infections are transmitted by direct contact, which may be sexual, but the incubation time ranges from weeks to more than a year. HPV DNA is widely distributed on skin in the general population. Infection may be subclinical.  Human papillomavirus (HPV) infects epithelial cells of skin and mucous membranes; $>150$ types of HPV have been identified. These cause infections at different sites (see Box 7.9). Infections are transmitted by direct contact, which may be sexual, but the incubation time ranges from weeks to more than a year. HPV DNA is widely distributed on skin in the general population. Infection may be subclinical.
$1^{circ}$ infections with HSV (usually type 1, occasionally type 2)  may be asymptomatic but can cause a painful ulcerative gingivostomatitis (most often in children) that resolves in 10-14 days. Recurrent herpes labialis is caused by reactivation of the latent virus (see $spadesuit$ p. 152). A  tingling, burning, or itching prodrome is common. Erythema multiforme may be caused by herpes infections and may recur (see $spadesuit$ p. 111).  $1^{\circ}$ infections with HSV (usually type 1, occasionally type 2)  may be asymptomatic but can cause a painful ulcerative gingivostomatitis (most often in children) that resolves in 10-14 days. Recurrent herpes labialis is caused by reactivation of the latent virus (see $\spadesuit$ p. 152). A  tingling, burning, or itching prodrome is common. Erythema multiforme may be caused by herpes infections and may recur (see $\spadesuit$ p. 111).  What should I look for in $1^{circ}$ infections?  What should I look for in $1^{\circ}$ infections?  •    Malaise, headache, and fever; cervical lymphadenopathy. · Well-defined vesicles, $2mathsf{m m}$ in diameter, on the dorsum of the tongue and hard palate, but vesicles may be scattered over the entire oral mucosa. Vesicles rupture rapidly to form very painful shallow ulcers with a yellowish grey floor and erythematous margins. •  Inflamed gingival margins.  •    Malaise, headache, and fever; cervical lymphadenopathy. · Well-defined vesicles, $2\mathsf{m m}$ in diameter, on the dorsum of the tongue and hard palate, but vesicles may be scattered over the entire oral mucosa. Vesicles rupture rapidly to form very painful shallow ulcers with a yellowish grey floor and erythematous margins. •  Inflamed gingival margins.  What should I do?  What should I do?  Management is symptomatic with fluids and pain relief. Oral aciclovir, $200mathrm{mg}times5/mathrm{d}$ ay for 5 days, is helpful in $1^{circ}$ attacks if vesicles are still present (within the first 3 days). Recurrent herpes labialis does not usually need treatment with aciclovir.  Management is symptomatic with fluids and pain relief. Oral aciclovir, $200\mathrm{mg}\times5/\mathrm{d}$ ay for 5 days, is helpful in $1^{\circ}$ attacks if vesicles are still present (within the first 3 days). Recurrent herpes labialis does not usually need treatment with aciclovir.

