Malignant melanoma accounts for $5%$ of skin cancers, but incidence in the UK has quadrupled since the 1970s $(>13300$ new cases in 2013). Melanoma is commoner in men than women, and almost 1/3 occur in people aged $<50$ (melanoma is the commonest cancer in young adults aged 15-34 years old). Superficial spreading melanoma (SSM) is the commonest type in Caucasians (see Box 17.8).  Malignant melanoma accounts for $5\%$ of skin cancers, but incidence in the UK has quadrupled since the 1970s $(>13300$ new cases in 2013). Melanoma is commoner in men than women, and almost 1/3 occur in people aged $<50$ (melanoma is the commonest cancer in young adults aged 15-34 years old). Superficial spreading melanoma (SSM) is the commonest type in Caucasians (see Box 17.8).  Most melanomas arise de novo; only $30-50%$ arise in pre-existing melanocytic naevi (usually SSM subtype). For risk factors, see $spadesuit$ pp. 346–7. Melanoma accounts for $75%$ of deaths associated with skin cancer.  Most melanomas arise de novo; only $30-50\%$ arise in pre-existing melanocytic naevi (usually SSM subtype). For risk factors, see $\spadesuit$ pp. 346–7. Melanoma accounts for $75\%$ of deaths associated with skin cancer.
